      "text": "### Frantz Fanon (1925-1961)"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#007",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  THE WRETCHED OF THE EARTH (1961):\n  Written as Algeria fought for independence; preface by Sartre.\n  Fanon was a Martinican psychiatrist; worked in Algeria."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#008",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "COLONIALISM AS TOTAL SYSTEM:\n  Colonialism is not just economic exploitation but a total social system.\n  It produces a \"colonized mind\" — internalization of inferiority.\n  The colonized learn to see themselves through the colonizer's eyes.\n  Violence of colonialism extends to the psyche (Black Skin, White Masks, 1952)."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#009",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "DECOLONIZATION:\n  Decolonization must be total — not just political independence\n  but a transformation of the colonized self.\n  Violence as purging: Fanon argued that revolutionary violence could\n  restore the colonized person's agency and dignity.\n  (Controversial; Arendt criticized as glorifying violence.)"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#010",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE PITFALLS OF NATIONAL CONSCIOUSNESS:\n  After independence: if the national bourgeoisie simply inherits the\n  colonial state structure, nothing changes.\n  The national middle class becomes a new exploiting class.\n  Fanon's warning about postcolonial elites proved prescient.\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#011",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
