        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "### Edward Said: Orientalism"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#012",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  EDWARD SAID (Orientalism, 1978):\n  The founding text of postcolonial literary studies."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#013",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE ARGUMENT:\n  \"Orientalism\" = a Western discourse about \"the Orient\" (Middle East, Asia).\n  Not a description of the Orient but a way of producing it as an object of knowledge.\n  The Orient is constructed as: irrational, sensual, despotic, timeless, feminine,\n  opposed to Western rationality, dynamism, freedom, masculinity."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#014",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "FOUCAULDIAN FRAMEWORK:\n  Said uses Foucault's concept of discourse.\n  Orientalism is a power-knowledge system: it supports colonial domination\n  while claiming to describe \"reality.\"\n  The discourse produces its object."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#015",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "EVIDENCE:\n  Novels (Flaubert, Kipling), scholarly texts, administrative reports, paintings.\n  Consistent representation of \"the Orient\" across different genres and periods.\n  Even sympathetic accounts reproduce Orientalist assumptions."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#016",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CRITICISMS:\n  Said's Orient is monolithic — different colonialists had different views.\n  Said didn't give enough agency to actual orientals.\n  Ignores Islamic and Asian scholarship and self-representation.\n  Presents \"the West\" as unified (it was not).\n  But: foundational for the field; critique of knowledge-power in scholarship."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#017",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CULTURE AND IMPERIALISM (1993):\n  Extended to literary canon: how English literature sustained and naturalized empire.\n  Jane Austen's Mansfield Park: the slave-owning property in Antigua that\n  funds the English estate goes unremarked. Said makes it visible.\n```"
    },
