      "text": "### Origins and Program"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#021",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  SUBALTERN STUDIES GROUP (1982):\n  Founded in India: Ranajit Guha, Shahid Amin, Partha Chatterjee,\n  David Hardiman, Sumit Sarkar, Gyanendra Pandey.\n  \"Subaltern\" from Gramsci: those subordinated by class, caste, gender, age."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#022",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "PROGRAM:\n  Write history of colonial India from the perspective of those\n  excluded from the nationalist/colonial archives.\n  Recover: peasant revolts, tribal uprisings, women's resistance,\n  subaltern consciousness."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#023",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "RANAJIT GUHA (Elementary Aspects of Peasant Insurgency, 1983):\n  Analyse 19th-century peasant revolts in India.\n  Peasant consciousness was not \"prepolitical\" (Hobsbawm's term).\n  Peasant action had its own rationality, codes, and objectives.\n  Reject the condescension that peasants \"don't understand\" politics."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#024",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "SOURCES:\n  Colonial administrative records (police reports, court records)\n  are the main sources — but they represent peasants through the\n  colonizer's lens.\n  Subaltern Studies read \"against the grain\": extract traces of\n  subaltern experience from hostile official sources.\n```"
    },
    {
