      },
      "text": "### Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak: \"Can the Subaltern Speak?\""
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#026",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  \"CAN THE SUBALTERN SPEAK?\" (1988):\n  The most famous and most debated article in postcolonial theory."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#027",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE ARGUMENT:\n  Can we recover the subaltern's voice from the historical record?\n  Spivak says: No — or at least, not without severe distortion."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#028",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE CASE: Sati (widow self-immolation) in colonial India.\n  Two \"sides\" in colonial discourse:\n  (a) British: \"white men saving brown women from brown men.\"\n  (b) Hindu nationalists: \"women choose to die for tradition.\"\n  The widow herself is absent from both narratives.\n  She cannot speak in the terms either discourse provides."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#029",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "IF THE SUBALTERN SPEAKS:\n  She is heard through existing discursive frameworks that distort her speech.\n  The intellectual who \"represents\" her also distorts.\n  There is no transparent access to the subaltern's consciousness."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#030",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "IMPLICATION (controversial):\n  This does not mean subalterns have no agency or voice.\n  It means: the production of the subaltern as a recoverable subject\n  is itself an effect of power, even in sympathetic scholarship.\n  Spivak's later work: responsibility of the scholar is to \"unlearn\"\n  privilege and to learn to listen without projecting."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md#031",
      "path": [
        "06-POSTCOLONIAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:social-history:06-postcolonial, git-history:social-history:06-postcolonial]",
        "canonical_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "current_path": "social-history/06-POSTCOLONIAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:social-history:postcolonial",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "social-history",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[postcolonial]",
        "section": "social-history",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Postcolonial and Subaltern History",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "RESPONSE:\n  Some historians: too pessimistic; oral history, material evidence,\n  practice theory can recover subaltern experience partially.\n  The warning stands: self-awareness about the position from which\n  you \"give voice\" to others.\n```"
    }
